speechtext:
All of these and the other provisions of the original treaty of 1961 have been fully preserved.
Now I come to the protocol. As I have indicated, the present government felt that it would be possible, while retaining the essentials of the treaty, to effect improvements that would remove points of doubt and, in some cases, meet specific criticisms that had been levied at the treaty as originally drafted. We have been able to achieve these goals. As the clarifications and improvements brought about
Columbia River Treaty in the protocol are of very real importance, I now propose to refer to them in some detail. The terms of the protocol itself are set forth, beginning at page 111, in the white paper.
Five of the paragraphs of the protocol deal in one way or another with the power benefits to which Canada is entitled under the treaty. Two of the provisions-paragraphs 8 and 10-increase the actual magnitude of the benefit in which Canada is entitled to share. Paragraph 8 provides that a 30 year period of stream flow record-from 1928 to 1958-shall be used as the basis of calculating the benefits derived from the Canadian storages. The treaty had provided for a 20 year period, from 1928 to 1948, unless otherwise agreed. The change that we have been able to effect is, of course, obviously of very definite economic advantage to Canada. The effect of including the additional ten years in the base of calculation is a substantial addition to our estimated energy benefits because in general the water flows in those ten years were higher than average, and consequently the benefits of storage are great. The net result is that the Canadian benefit is increased by approximately 500 million kilowatt hours annually. This amounts to an increase of about 18 per cent.
Paragraph 10 removes a doubt as to whether the electricity required for pumping at the Grand Coulee project in the United States is to be included as part of the load served there. It is to be included and the result is to add between 5 and 7 per cent to the Canadian credit in electrical capacity.
A criticism that had been directed at the treaty was that it gave the United States too great a degree of control over the operation of Canadian storages for power. Well, let us see what the protocol does about that. Actually, the treaty provides for joint operating plans which take into account the requirements of Canada for control of storages for our own power purposes as well as the requirements for downstream power. However, it appeared that there might be some room for doubt. The protocol removes any uncertainty in this regard by making the operating plans subject to the following conditions:
(a)	The plans will commit only the amount of Canadian treaty storage necessary to produce the downstream benefits credited to that storage;
(b)	The plans will be based on the same series of studies from which the benefits are calculated five years in advance;
(c)	Canada is given full discretion to decide from which projects storage releases will be made;
(d)	Canada is given full discretion also in the detailed operation which will give the monthly storage quantities required by the agreed operating plan drawn up five years in advance.
